<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Isin</h1><p class="st-meta">2017 BCE – 1794 BCE (approx.) · capital Isin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom founded by a former official of Ur that claimed the Sumerian imperial heritage after 2004 BCE and produced the law code of Lipit-Ishtar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishbi-erra">Ishbi-Erra</a>, an official from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a>, last king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, made himself independent at Isin around 2017 BCE as Ur's grip failed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> sacked Ur in 2004 BCE, he expelled their garrison and presented his dynasty as the legitimate successor of Ur, controlling <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nippur">Nippur</a>, Ur and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Isin's kings kept up Sumerian court culture: they wrote royal hymns, copied Sumerian literature and claimed divinity like the kings of Ur. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lipit-ishtar">Lipit-Ishtar</a> issued a law code in Sumerian around 1930 BCE. The city goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninisina">Ninisina</a> was a healer, and archaeologists found a cemetery of dogs, her sacred animal. A famous later story tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erra-imitti">Erra-imitti</a> installed a gardener, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil-bani">Enlil-bani</a>, as substitute king, only to die, leaving Enlil-bani on the throne.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Isin lost Ur and the Gulf trade to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/larsa">Larsa</a> around 1932 BCE and became one city-state among several. In 1794 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> conquered it. Isin itself revived later, giving its name to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-dynasty-of-isin">Second Dynasty of Isin</a> in the 12th century BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 2017 BCE – 1794 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:19.56%;width:4.44%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2017 BCE – 1794 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Isin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ishbi-Erra, Lipit-Ishtar, Enlil-bani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Dynasty of Isin, Isin dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1906&amp;focus=isin" aria-label="Isin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5846 1016 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Isin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6166 1272L6193 1305M6166 1272L6143 1252M6166 1272L6184 1294"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6193 1305h0M6143 1252h0M6184 1294h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6166 1272h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6166 1272h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1906&amp;focus=isin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
